(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatology-ii-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Dermatology II Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is beremagene geperpavec?","choices":["A monoclonal antibody given intravenously for junctional epidermolysis bullosa","An oral small molecule that restores keratin 5 and 14 function","A non-adherent biological dressing designed for fragile skin","A topical gene therapy gel approved in 2023 for dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, accessible through dermatology referral centres and gene therapy trials. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which points should the conversation with a hyperhidrosis patient cover?","choices":["That it is not about poor hygiene or anxiety, with moisture-wicking clothing suggested and realistic expectations set","That improved washing and a reduction in stress will resolve all of the sweating within a few months","That a single course of treatment produces a permanent cure","That the condition is rare and few treatment options exist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — botulinum toxin needs repeating every three to six months and can be cost-prohibitive. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Why must malignancy and infection be excluded in a rapidly expanding ulcer before treating it as pyoderma gangrenosum?","choices":["Because a biopsy cannot be taken once immunosuppression has started","Because the diagnosis is one of exclusion and the immunosuppression used would worsen an infection","Because pyoderma gangrenosum is itself a premalignant condition","Because antibiotics would become the first-line treatment once an infection is confirmed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the question to answer is why this ulcer is suddenly expanding. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which measure is specifically advised against in the management of sunburn?","choices":["Popping blisters, because intact blisters are protective","Starting a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug early in the course","Applying aloe vera or a soy-based topical moisturiser","Giving oral or intravenous fluids for systemic symptoms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — cool rather than cold water is also specified. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which advice is given about blisters in second-degree sunburn?","choices":["Apply a high-potency topical corticosteroid directly to them","Cover them with silver sulfadiazine to prevent colonisation","Do not pop them, because intact blisters are protective","Drain them under sterile conditions to reduce infection risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with cool compresses and early nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for pain. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which systemic therapy has the strongest current evidence in toxic epidermal necrolysis?","choices":["Prophylactic antibiotics covering skin and gut flora","Cyclosporine at 3 to 5 mg/kg daily, started early","Intravenous immunoglobulin at 1 g/kg daily for three days","High-dose systemic corticosteroids given for the first week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with intravenous immunoglobulin and etanercept as adjunct options. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which systemic complications are named in toxic epidermal necrolysis?","choices":["Neurocutaneous melanosis, hydrocephalus and seizures in early childhood","Symblepharon alone, with no involvement of any other organ system","Sepsis, acute respiratory distress syndrome, acute kidney injury, electrolyte imbalance, hypovolaemia and gastrointestinal haemorrhage","Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, acute rheumatic fever and a post-infectious reactive arthritis of the knee"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — survivors also face sicca syndrome, lung disease and psychological trauma. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"How is the diagnosis of pyoderma gangrenosum established?","choices":["By exclusion, since there is no gold standard test","By biopsy showing a pathognomonic palisading granuloma","By wound culture growing a characteristic organism","By serum antibody testing against dermal structural proteins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — biopsy from the ulcer edge shows dense neutrophils and rules out malignancy and infection. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which referral is indicated for generalised granuloma annulare in an older adult?","choices":["Ophthalmology, if any ocular symptoms have developed","Oncology, if lymphoma is suspected","Vascular surgery, if venous reflux is suspected","Thoracic surgery, if refractory disease is present"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside dermatology for phototherapy or systemic therapy and endocrine workup for diabetes or thyroid disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What kind of process is erythema nodosum, histologically?","choices":["A palisading granuloma around degenerating collagen","A dense neutrophilic infiltrate without organisms","A septal panniculitis without vasculitis","A lobular panniculitis with associated vasculitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the most common form of panniculitis, a delayed hypersensitivity reaction in subcutaneous fat. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is field cancerisation, and how does it change management?","choices":["Clinically normal skin around the lesions harbours subclinical mutations, so multiple or confluent lesions need field-directed therapy","Lesions spread outward from a single origin, so wide excision is required","Lesions seed distant sites through lymphatics, so staging is required","Lesions recur at exactly the same site after treatment, so repeat cryotherapy at regular intervals is the appropriate response"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — 5-fluorouracil, imiquimod, tirbanibulin or diclofenac treat the field rather than single lesions. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which test is the gold standard for diagnosing dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["Lesional skin biopsy with haematoxylin and eosin showing subepidermal blistering with neutrophils","Serum immunoglobulin A anti-tissue transglutaminase with anti-endomysial antibodies","Small bowel biopsy demonstrating villous atrophy consistent with coeliac disease","Perilesional skin biopsy with direct immunofluorescence showing granular immunoglobulin A in the dermal papillae"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Immunoglobulin A anti-epidermal transglutaminase is the most specific serological test. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which counselling point is emphasised for acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["It responds fully to topical antifungal therapy within several weeks","It resolves spontaneously in most patients without any intervention","It is a metabolic warning sign rather than a hygiene problem, and 5 to 10% weight loss improves the findings","It is a benign cosmetic change that carries no systemic implication and requires no further investigation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the hygiene misconception is specifically addressed. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which features separate a fixed drug eruption from erythema multiforme?","choices":["It produces wheals that migrate, blanch fully and resolve within twenty-four hours of first appearing","It produces extensive epidermal detachment with mucosal involvement and systemic toxicity","It produces tender nodules on the anterior shins that never go on to ulcerate","It recurs at the same site, leaves residual hyperpigmentation, and has few lesions without three concentric zones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the three-zone target is what defines an erythema multiforme lesion. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which haplotypes are strongly associated with dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8","HLA-B*15:02 and HLA-B*58:01","HLA-Cw6 and HLA-B27","HLA-A31 and HLA-BW35"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and nearly all patients have underlying gluten-sensitive enteropathy even when gastrointestinally asymptomatic. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A patient's facial erythema could be rosacea or lupus and the picture is unclear. Which test helps, and how should the result be read?","choices":["An antinuclear antibody, which helps rule autoimmune disease OUT — a positive result only means more testing is needed","An antinuclear antibody, which rules autoimmune disease IN when positive and needs no further testing","A skin biopsy of the malar area, which distinguishes the two definitively","A potassium hydroxide preparation of the affected facial skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — many things make it positive, so it cannot rule a condition in. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What is the gold standard test for determining the cleavage plane in epidermolysis bullosa?","choices":["Serum enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay for structural protein antibodies","Skin biopsy with transmission electron microscopy","Skin biopsy with direct immunofluorescence for immunoglobulin G","Genetic testing of the known structural protein genes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with immunofluorescence antigen mapping used to localise missing or reduced proteins. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the expected course of localised granuloma annulare?","choices":["Half of cases resolve spontaneously within two years, so watchful waiting is reasonable","Nearly all cases progress without systemic therapy, so early treatment is essential","Most cases transform into squamous cell carcinoma if left untreated","Lesions persist unchanged lifelong and never resolve without intervention"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — treatment is primarily for cosmesis or discomfort, and there is no malignant potential. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What distinguishes solar lentigo from lentigo maligna?","choices":["Solar lentigo appears in early childhood; lentigo maligna appears only after the age of seventy","Solar lentigo appears on covered skin; lentigo maligna on exposed skin","Solar lentigo has uniform pigmentation; lentigo maligna is asymmetric with irregular border and colour","Solar lentigo is raised and rough; lentigo maligna is flat and smooth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and dermoscopy is essential — solar lentigo shows finger-like projections and a moth-eaten border. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"What drives the commonest, insulin-resistant form of acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["Autoimmune destruction of the melanocytes within the affected intertriginous skin","Chronic ultraviolet exposure driving proliferation of the basal layer melanocytes and increased melanin production","Immunoglobulin A deposition in the dermal papillae activating neutrophils","Hyperinsulinaemia stimulating keratinocyte and fibroblast proliferation through insulin-like growth factor 1 receptor cross-activation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Malignant, drug-induced and endocrine forms make up the rest. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the immunological basis of dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["Immunoglobulin G antibodies against desmoglein between the keratinocytes","A T-cell mediated destruction of melanocytes within the affected epidermis","Immunoglobulin A antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase, forming complexes that deposit in the dermal papillae","Immunoglobulin G antibodies directed against the epidermal basement membrane zone at the dermal junction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — complement and neutrophils are then activated, producing subepidermal blisters. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How much do tanning beds reduce risk compared with natural sun, and how are they classified?","choices":["They halve ultraviolet B exposure while delivering the same ultraviolet A dose","They are safer for Fitzpatrick types I and II under supervision","They are acceptable if limited to the months outside peak summer","They are not safer at all, and are classified as Group 1 carcinogens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — there is no safe level of tanning. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"What is the most common infectious trigger of erythema nodosum?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Group A Streptococcus","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Herpes simplex virus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside Yersinia, tuberculosis, coccidioidomycosis, histoplasmosis and hepatitis B and C. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which intervention is described as the single most evidence-supported for photoaging?","choices":["Chemical peeling with glycolic or trichloroacetic acid","Daily broad-spectrum sunscreen at SPF 30 or above","Ablative laser resurfacing performed every few years","Daily topical vitamin C at 10 to 20% concentration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, for both preventing and slowing progression, and beginning in childhood gives maximal lifetime benefit. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Which type of epidermolysis bullosa is most common, and what is its cleavage level?","choices":["Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa, at about 70% of cases, with sub-lamina densa cleavage","Kindler epidermolysis bullosa, at about 70% of cases, with mixed cleavage planes","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex, at about 70% of cases, with intraepidermal cleavage","Junctional epidermolysis bullosa, at about 70% of cases, with cleavage in the lamina lucida"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it results from keratin 5 and 14 mutations and is autosomal dominant. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is the hardening phenomenon in polymorphous light eruption?","choices":["Loss of pigment in the affected areas after each episode","Improvement through the summer as tolerance develops after repeated exposure","Thickening and hardening of the skin from repeated scratching of the eruption","Progressive scarring of affected sites over successive seasons"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is also why prophylactic narrowband ultraviolet B in spring is the most effective preventive strategy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which single intervention most improves survival in Stevens-Johnson syndrome?","choices":["Early administration of intravenous immunoglobulin at 1 g/kg daily for three days","Prophylactic broad-spectrum antibiotics started on admission","High-dose systemic corticosteroids given throughout the admission","Immediate withdrawal of the causative drug, since each day of delay worsens prognosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — earlier withdrawal is strongly associated with improved survival. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"A patient with chronic urticaria is still symptomatic on a standard dose of a non-sedating antihistamine. What comes next?","choices":["Stop the antihistamine and start a leukotriene antagonist alone","Increase to up to four times the standard dose, and consider adding an H2 blocker","Switch immediately to a long-term oral corticosteroid taken daily","Switch across to a first-generation sedating antihistamine at standard dose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — omalizumab and ciclosporin follow if that fails. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which drugs are named as causes of acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["Niacin, corticosteroids, oral contraceptives and protease inhibitors","Carbamazepine, phenytoin, lamotrigine and allopurinol preparations","Doxycycline, ciprofloxacin, amiodarone and the thiazide diuretics","Lithium, systemic steroids and acnegenic mineral oil exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — drug-induced disease sits alongside the insulin-resistant and malignant forms. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which feature separates photodermatitis from seborrhoeic dermatitis on the face?","choices":["Photodermatitis involves the nasolabial folds, which seborrhoeic dermatitis spares","Photodermatitis produces greasy yellow scale, which seborrhoeic dermatitis lacks","Photodermatitis is confined to the scalp, which seborrhoeic dermatitis spares","Photodermatitis spares the nasolabial folds, which seborrhoeic dermatitis involves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — shadowed areas are protected from light. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 101"}]};
